# Egress restriction for every Pod in the "default" namespace -- this is
# where every SpinApp (JS/Proxy/static-site) and every CronJob deployed via
# the console lands, i.e. every workload an authenticated console user
# (potentially any Keycloak-realm user, unless /config's console access
# allow-list is populated) can create the *content* of. Without this, such
# a workload can reach anything the node itself can reach: every other
# namespace's ClusterIP services (the k8s API server, cert-manager's
# webhook, spin-operator's webhook, metrics-server), the node's own other
# ports, and -- if this cluster is ever run on a cloud VM -- the cloud
# metadata endpoint (169.254.169.254) for IAM credential theft. See
# docs/TECHNICAL_NOTES.md's "Egress hardening" for the fuller writeup of
# why this was needed even though JS/Proxy apps' Wasm sandbox and the
# app-level checks (check_backend_host_not_internal() for Proxy apps)
# already narrow this somewhat -- this is the actual enforcement layer,
# since Wasm's allowed_outbound_hosts is wide open by design and
# app-level checks can't see what a hostname resolves to at request time.
#
# policyTypes is Egress only -- Ingress is untouched, so Traefik (a
# different namespace) can still reach every app's Service normally.
#
# Scoped to this cluster's own Pod CIDR + Service CIDR (where the actual
# cluster-internal pivot targets live: the k8s API, webhooks,
# metrics-server, other apps' ClusterIPs) plus link-local/metadata and
# loopback -- NOT the full RFC1918 space. Blocking all of RFC1918 would
# also cut off Proxy apps' Backend Origin pointing at a legitimate LAN
# backend (e.g. a NAS, an internal API, a self-hosted service elsewhere
# on the LAN) -- that's a real, intentional use of the Proxy feature, not
# something to treat as equivalent to reaching the cluster's own
# internals. The container registry (also on this node's LAN IP,
# port 5000) stays inaccessible from pods regardless of this policy's
# ranges, because registry.yaml binds it to 127.0.0.1 only -- that fix,
# not this NetworkPolicy, is what protects it, which is why leaving the
# wider LAN open here doesn't reopen that specific risk.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: spinapp-egress-restrict
  namespace: default
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes:
    - Egress
  egress:
    # DNS must be explicitly allowed -- kube-dns's ClusterIP (10.43.0.10)
    # would otherwise fall inside the 10.0.0.0/8 block below, and without
    # this every other egress rule becomes useless (nothing resolves).
    #
    # Matched by CoreDNS's actual Pod (namespaceSelector + podSelector),
    # NOT by an ipBlock on the Service ClusterIP -- verified empirically
    # in this cluster that an ipBlock: 10.43.0.10/32 rule silently never
    # matches (DNS stayed broken even with that rule present), almost
    # certainly because this CNI's egress enforcement sees the packet
    # after kube-proxy's ClusterIP->PodIP DNAT, by which point the
    # destination is CoreDNS's actual Pod IP, not the ClusterIP anymore.
    # A label selector matches the real Pod IP regardless of DNAT, and
    # survives CoreDNS's Pod IP changing across restarts/rescheduling.
    - to:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              k8s-app: kube-dns
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 53
    # Everything else: allow the public internet AND the wider LAN
    # (legitimate Proxy-app use), deny only this cluster's own Pod/Service
    # CIDRs plus link-local/metadata/loopback/this node's own IP.
    # 10.42.0.0/16 is k3s's default cluster-wide Pod CIDR (this single
    # node's own slice is 10.42.0.0/24, but the /16 is blocked in case
    # more nodes join later); 10.43.0.0/16 is the Service CIDR (DNS to
    # kube-dns's Pod excepted above). Note this leaves the *rest* of
    # 10.0.0.0/8, all of 172.16.0.0/12 (including whatever /24 this
    # node's own LAN segment happens to fall in), and all of
    # 192.168.0.0/16 reachable -- see the header comment above for why
    # that's an intentional tradeoff, not an oversight.
    #
    # __NODE_IP__/32 (this node's own LAN IP, substituted by install.sh
    # from `ip -4 route get 1.1.1.1` at install time -- see that script)
    # is excepted-from-the-except (i.e. still blocked) despite otherwise
    # allowing this node's own /24: k3s's own apiserver isn't a Pod, so
    # `kubernetes.default.svc`'s ClusterIP (10.43.0.1, nominally already
    # blocked above) actually DNATs to this node's real IP on :6443 --
    # confirmed live that this bypassed the Service-CIDR block entirely
    # (same DNAT-vs-NetworkPolicy ordering issue as the DNS rule above),
    # so the *apiserver* only stays blocked as long as this specific /32
    # does too. This also means any other host-bound port (not just the
    # registry, which additionally protects itself by binding
    # 127.0.0.1-only) is unreachable from pods.
    #
    # Multi-node clusters: add one `- <node-ip>/32` line per node here
    # (install.sh only substitutes the node it runs on).
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: 0.0.0.0/0
            except:
              - 10.42.0.0/16
              - 10.43.0.0/16
              - 169.254.0.0/16
              - 127.0.0.0/8
              - __NODE_IP__/32
    # This whole IPv6 rule (up to the matching end-marker comment below)
    # is removed entirely by install.sh on a single-stack (IPv4-only, the
    # default) install; kept only when SPINDECK_DUAL_STACK was used, with
    # the node-IPv6 placeholder substituted the same way the IPv4 one is
    # above. This exists at all because a dual-stack Pod can actually
    # originate IPv6 egress traffic -- the IPv4-only ipBlock above has no
    # effect on it whatsoever, so without this the whole point of this
    # NetworkPolicy (containing self-service code/config as a network
    # pivot) would have an IPv6-shaped hole in it on any dual-stack
    # install.
    # __IPV6_BLOCK_START__
    - to:
        - ipBlock:
            cidr: ::/0
            except:
              - fd42::/56       # Pod CIDR (IPv6) -- see install.sh
              - fd43::/112      # Service CIDR (IPv6) -- see install.sh
              - fe80::/10       # link-local
              - ::1/128         # loopback
              - __NODE_IPV6__/128
    # __IPV6_BLOCK_END__
